Situation:
The Corps is sustaining multiple forward battalion and squadron elements operating across a contested valley and adjacent highlands. Rear-area stocks are staged at several depots (e.g., Supply Depot Alpha, Bravo, Charlie), pushing a single critical supply class forward to units at FOBs and COPs (e.g., 3rd Infantry Battalion at FOB Ridge, 2nd Cavalry Squadron at COP Sabre, 12th Engineers at COP Hammer). Valley routes are under observation and subject to IED threat; mountain passes are narrow and weather-limited. Movement requires convoy security, engineer route clearance, and deconfliction with ongoing maneuver. Demand and depot availability are time-phased across the distribution window.

Mission:
Deliver required supplies from depots to forward units over the planning horizon while minimizing total operational cost, comprised of:
- Transport costs along each depot-to-unit route.
- Shortage penalties for any unmet unit demand.
- Vehicle deployment costs.
- Fixed route security risk costs for each activated route-day.

Execution/Concept:
- Depot capacity: On each day, outbound shipments from a depot cannot exceed its available stocks. Flows will be synchronized with daily receipts and drawdowns to prevent over-issue.
- Demand satisfaction: Each forward unit has a daily requirement. Shortfalls are permissible only as a last resort and will incur a penalty cost; planners will prioritize high-risk and time-sensitive units to avoid operational degradation.
- Vehicle capacity and deployment: Loads are constrained by per-vehicle capacity and the available fleet. For each depot-to-unit lane and day, the total shipped volume cannot exceed the product of vehicle capacity and the number of vehicles assigned to that lane. Vehicle deployment incurs a per-vehicle cost.
- Route activation and risk: Activating a route (sending any vehicles on a depot–unit lane on a given day) triggers a fixed Risk Cost, representing escorts, route clearance, ISR coverage, and C2 overhead—this cost applies regardless of how many vehicles are used on that activated route-day. To control overhead and exposure, planners will consolidate loads on fewer activated routes when feasible.
- Fleet limit: The total number of vehicles employed on any day cannot exceed the available fleet. Convoy timings will account for daylight, weather, and deconfliction with maneuver and engineer clearance windows.
- Cost-minimizing approach: Favor secure and efficient lanes; maximize vehicle utilization on activated routes to amortize fixed risk costs; preserve depot drawdown rates within limits; and accept measured shortfalls only where the penalty is lower than the combined transport, vehicle, and risk costs. Routes through exposed valleys may require activation only when the operational benefit justifies the fixed security overhead.

